WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.940
Box office success for a Marvel film is no
big surprise, but the numbers for Spider-Man:

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.849
Far From Home are well above even the hefty
expectations that had been set for Spidey's

00:00:08.849 --> 00:00:11.620
second solo swing through the Marvel Cinematic
Universe.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.520
Here are some reasons why it soared.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.700
And if you haven't seen it yet, here's your
warning: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.199
Far From Home basks in what you might call
an "Endgame aura."

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:24.490
Coming just two short months after the Avengers'
climactic time heist broke the opening weekend

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:29.249
record to the tune of $1.2 billion, Far From
Home promised to serve as an epilogue to 11

00:00:29.249 --> 00:00:30.500
years of Marvel films.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.940
A preview of Far From Home was added to the
Endgame re-release in the place of an after-credits

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:34.920
scene.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.840
The re-release effectively stirred the waters,
bringing Marvel back into the forefront of

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.149
moviegoers' minds, teasing that Far From Home
promised some juicy answers to those lingering

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:44.340
Endgame mysteries.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.400
Endgame juggled a lot of different characters
and storylines...

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.440
"Is that everyone?"

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:49.320
"You wanted more?"

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.780
...so a relatively low-key film like Far From
Home helped answer some basic questions.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.399
It showed what life was like in the aftermath
of the "Blip" — the characters' term for

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.820
the second snap that brought everyone back
in Endgame.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.580
It addressed the age differences caused by
the five-year gap and showed how the world

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.460
was handling the loss of Tony Stark.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.160
People quite simply love Peter Parker.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.300
The Web-Head is one of the most popular superheroes
of all time, having served as Marvel Comics'

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.510
flagship character for more than 50 years,
and holding down his own movie franchises

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:17.510
for nearly 20.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.860
Andrew Garfield did a compelling, if sobering
job as Peter Parker in his pair of Amazing

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:26.140
Spider-Man films, and the web-slinging wonder
boy Tobey Maguire will always hold a nostalgic

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:27.160
place for fans.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.120
But ever since he first appeared in Captain
America: Civil War, Tom Holland has clearly

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.280
created one of the most popular versions of
Spider-Man to date.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.120
He just seems to have a charisma people can't
get enough of.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.940
Spider-Man: Far From Home was released on
a Tuesday, which gave it a lengthy, six-day

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.800
"opening weekend" in the midst of the busy
summer movie season.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.000
The release date had originally been Friday,
July 5th, but Sony made the wise decision

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.880
to move that date up to before the Independence
Day holiday.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.800
The move was meant to avoid any negative impacts
from the movie debuting around a holiday,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.360
an arrangement which can cause a dip in ticket
sales.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.360
The shift to Tuesday helped the movie gain
momentum heading into the 4th of July, and

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:03.540
it paid off in spades.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.600
Not only did the movie hit a home run at the
box office, but it broke both Tuesday and

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.500
Wednesday ticket sales records, even beating
out Endgame in the process.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.080
Far From Home served as the finale not only
to MCU Phase 3, but also to the Infinity Saga

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:17.840
as a whole.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.120
Marvel President Kevin Feige explained why
Spidey's story needed to be the bookend that

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.600
showed what came after Tony Stark's death
in Endgame:

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.980
"The relationship between Peter Parker and
Tony Stark is so special that we needed to

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.940
see how does Spider-Man step out of the shadow
of his mentor, Tony Stark."

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.260
Far From Home wasn't just a follow up to Endgame;
it was literally the final chapter in a twenty-two-film

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.300
story, making it a must-see experience.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.480
The film is also the last MCU movie scheduled
to release for nearly 10 months, so MCU fans

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.219
looking for their superhero fix knew this
was their last chance to get one for a bit

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.120
— from Marvel Studios, at least.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:56.100
Brought to life by Jake Gyllenhaal, Mysterio
is a spectacular combination of a compelling

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.860
background, sinister motives, and twisted
logic.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.120
He's a villain with depth and motivation that
resonates with the viewers, in part because

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.220
he's so sympathetic and likable in the first
half of the movie.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.739
Even the trailers spun him as a hero from
another dimension, a sort of second Iron Man.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.360
Beck's evil motives weren't a sure bet going
into the film.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.660
After all, just a few months before, Marvel
had pulled a 180 with the Skrulls in Captain

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:21.420
Marvel, turning the classic comic book villains
into tragic protagonists on the screen.

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:29.079
"My people lived as refugees on Torfa, homeless
ever since we resisted Kree rule and they

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.280
destroyed our planet."

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.740
While Beck ultimately showed his true colors,
his loyalties were an open question, and one

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.240
that likely pushed many viewers to head to
theaters looking for an answer.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.219
It was widely reported that the late Stan
Lee's last cameo had come in Endgame.

00:03:42.219 --> 00:03:46.150
That turned out to be true, but that didn't
mean other cameos would be left by the wayside

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:47.150
in Far From Home.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:50.390
A key question for many fans was just who
would be showing up in the film.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:54.390
Given his appearance in the trailers, Nick
Fury's involvement was a given, but one surprise

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.599
is that he and Maria Hill turn out to be Skrulls
in disguise while the real Fury is taking

00:03:58.599 --> 00:04:03.049
a vacation, with Ben Mendelsohn and Sharon
Blynn reprising their roles as Talos and Soren

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:04.100
from Captain Marvel.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:08.920
In another interesting twist, Peter Billingsley
reprises his role as William Ginter Riva,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.680
the scientist confronted by Obadiah Stane
in Iron Man.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:15.620
And, of course, the best surprise of all had
to be J.K. Simmons' return to his role as

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:18.180
J. Jonah Jameson during the mid-credits scene.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.180
"You don't trust anybody.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.120
That's your problem."

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:21.440
"I trust my barber."

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.340
Fans knew that the creators at Marvel wouldn't
let them down in the cameo department, and

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:27.840
sure enough, they came through with flying
colors.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:33.000
Far From Home clearly functioned as a capstone
for the Infinity Saga, but it also promised

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.640
to deliver a taste of what was to come next
in the MCU.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.920
How would it continue without Tony Stark and
Steve Rogers at the helm?

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:42.040
Would Peter Parker end up stepping into Iron
Man's boots, and how?

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.560
Would the world be any different — or even
make sense — after the snap or the "blip"

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:46.280
that followed?

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:47.420
What about the multiverse?

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.180
"The snap tore a hole in our dimension."

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.140
"You're saying there's a multiverse?"

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.260
The desire for answers to all these questions
along with a glimpse at how Marvel was setting

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.310
things up for Phase 4 was certainly on the
minds of many fans as they bought their tickets

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:02.160
— more than once for some, it seems.

